# ssd1306_slave: a mono OLED controller's protocol and display memory, as
# a slave — so that a part can stand in for the glass.
#
# It takes **bytes with a command/data tag**, not wires. The transport is
# somebody else's job: `ip/bus/spi_display_rx` gives it four-wire SPI,
# which is what an SSD1306 in 4-wire mode speaks, and an I2C or parallel
# front end would give it the same stream. Splitting the protocol from the
# wire is what makes the block testable without a serial link and usable
# behind any other one.
#
# What it is for: developing and watching a display driver without the
# panel, and capturing the pixels a driver would have drawn.
# `frame_done` strobes when a whole frame has landed — the pointer wrapping
# to the start of both windows, which needs no idle timer and no guess
# about rate — so a host can take a copy after each redraw.
#
# The memory is an SSD1306's, which is **pages and not scanlines**: each
# byte is eight vertically stacked pixels, bit 0 the topmost, so pixel
# (x, y) is bit y%8 of address (y/8)*COLUMNS + x. 128x64 is 1024 bytes and
# fits one `RAMB18E1` with half left over. Nothing is transposed here,
# because a scanline layout is a format no SSD1306 driver speaks.
#
# Geometry is parameterised: PAGES=8 is 128x64, PAGES=4 the 128x32 part,
# and COLUMNS=132 an SH1106, which is the same protocol over a wider
# memory.
#
# **The command set is QUOTED** from the Solomon Systech SSD1306 datasheet
# revision 1.1, section 10's command table and section 8.7 on the display
# RAM. It has not been checked against a real SSD1306 — standing in for
# one is the point — and **no part has run this block**. What is checked is
# that it behaves as the datasheet describes: `rtl/ssd1306_slave_tb.v`
# drives the initialisation sequence a real driver sends, writes a full
# frame, and reads all 1024 bytes back at addresses it computes itself.
#
# That testbench checks the block's **accounting** before its pixels —
# every counter against a driven total, including the count before
# anything is driven at all. That order is deliberate:
# `ip/bus/spi_display_rx` shipped with two defects that masked each other
# and left one counter permanently one too high, and fifteen of its own
# testbenches missed it because not one compared a counter against what it
# had driven.
#
# `unknown_cmds` counts a command this block does not implement and
# otherwise ignores it, as a real SSD1306 does with most of the reserved
# space — so a driver using something unmodelled is a number somebody can
# read rather than a silent misbehaviour.
name ssd1306_slave
version 1.0.0
license MIT
description "An SSD1306-compatible mono display as a slave: the command set, the page-organised display memory, a readable frame buffer and a strobe when a frame is complete"

top ssd1306_slave
target ice40
target ecp5
target xc7

source rtl/ssd1306_slave.v

param COLUMNS int 128 1..512
param PAGES int 8 1..32
param COUNT_WIDTH int 16 1..32

port clk in
port rst_n in
port in_byte in 8
port in_is_data in
port in_valid in
port rd_addr in 16
port rd_data out 8
port frame_done out
port display_on out
port inverse out
port all_on out
port contrast out 8
port addr_mode out 2
port charge_pump out
port seg_remap out
port com_reverse out
port col_ptr out 8
port page_ptr out 8
port pending_args out 2
port cmd_count out 16
port data_count out 16
port frame_count out 16
port unknown_cmds out 16
